"The same node types in a graph that declares no federation link. Federation is opt-in and absence is the default, so nothing is synthesized however many nodes the graph declares; what an author wrote still composes, an authored key being a fact about the type rather than a consequence of the opt-in. Stated over the composition rather than over the synthesis, because absence is only assertable as a population that holds everything else: a derived row would arrive here with no ordinal, and none does. Resolvable is left out of the claim because an authored key that does not spell it records absence rather than the default, which is a separate fact from the one this document is about."
type Film @table(name: "film") @node @key(fields: "title") {
  id: ID
  title: String
}

# A node with no key of its own, which in a linked graph would be given one.
type Language @table(name: "language") @node {
  id: ID
  name: String
}

extend type Query {
  films: [Film!]!
  languages: [Language!]!
}

extend schema @expectEquals(relation: "intent_federation_key", rows: """
  type_name, ordinal, fields_sdl
  Film,      0,       title
  """)
